Highball: a client surface for a window another process owns draws into an overlay window.

DXMT (and D3DMetal) reach the driver through the macdrv_functions shim: get_win_data(hwnd) ->
client_cocoa_view -> a Metal layer. A window created by another process has no win data and no
Cocoa view in this process, so the shim returned NULL and a cross-process swapchain (a browser's
GPU process presenting into the browser window, which is how Steam's, Rockstar's and Ubisoft's
CEF work) could not exist. The shim now creates the client surface anyway and gives it a
borderless, non-activating, mouse-transparent overlay window of ours, framed on the foreign
window's client rect and ordered directly above the foreign window (found by owner pid and frame
through CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo) on every present; hidden while the foreign window is hidden
or minimized. DXMT's side is the DXMT_ALLOW_CROSS_PROCESS_SWAPCHAIN opt-in in Highball's DXMT
branch. Known limits: the overlay is a separate window to the compositor (no clipping to the
foreign window's shape, ordering during Mission Control), and the surface is kept alive for the
life of the process.

